1
00:00:00,003 --> 00:00:03,523
The Learning Creature
Can a random brain learn to find food?

2
00:00:03,792 --> 00:00:08,002
This creature starts with a random brain: 206 untrained weights.

3
00:00:08,269 --> 00:00:12,413
Green is food. Grey blocks the way. Red drains energy. It must eat to survive.

4
00:00:12,749 --> 00:00:15,608
A genetic algorithm evolves the brain: 40 creatures, 50 generations.

5
00:00:15,695 --> 00:00:23,309
Live training on 8 practice worlds. Nothing here is scripted.

6
00:00:23,397 --> 00:00:36,323
Each generation keeps the best brains, then mixes and mutates them.

7
00:00:36,409 --> 00:00:45,605
The chart plots real fitness scores from the simulation.

8
00:00:45,861 --> 00:00:49,171
Trained and evaluated in 91 seconds on a CPU (shown as a time-lapse).

9
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:54,611
Same world, never seen in training. Left: untrained. Right: trained.

10
00:00:54,874 --> 00:00:59,805
Same creature. Same challenge. Different learned behavior.

11
00:01:00,719 --> 00:01:06,608
On 30 held-out worlds, food found rose from 0.26 to 5.57 of 6 (95% CI of the gain: 4.79 to 5.69).

12
00:01:06,918 --> 00:01:13,202
Limits: it only matches a one-line "go to the nearest food" rule (5.77), and it did not learn to avoid hazards.

13
00:01:13,208 --> 00:01:17,013
Real learning, honestly measured
Food-seeking: learned.  Hazard avoidance: not shown.
